-- Retired: laplace.constituent_edges (table), constituent_edges_rebuild(int) (procedure),
-- and constituents_closure_edges(bytea[], int) (the closure that read the table).
--
-- The whole subsystem was a second copy of a fact the substrate already stores exactly.
-- physicalities.trajectory IS the constituent sequence -- Trajectory.Build packs the ordered
-- child ids into the linestring mantissa and laplace_trajectory_constituents unpacks them
-- losslessly. realize.constituents() and realize.constituents_closure() read that trajectory directly, and
-- so does the live render path. constituent_edges held a decode of the same bytes in a
-- different shape, and its own rebuild header said so: "it only unpacks what
-- physicalities.trajectory already encodes."
--
-- It could never be trusted, because nothing maintained it. Ingest did not write it; the
-- only writer was a manual TRUNCATE-and-rebuild procedure invoked by CALL. On a fully
-- seeded database it held ZERO rows, which is the failure mode of derived-but-hand-refreshed
-- state: silently empty, and anything reading it silently returns nothing. The rebuild
-- comment anticipated a "(separately validated) cutover" that never landed, so after the
-- cutover never happened the only consumer left was its own regress test asserting that the
-- edges-based closure agrees with the trajectory-based one it was meant to replace.
--
-- The access pattern it was reaching for -- child->parent, or "which parents start with this
-- child" -- is real. The answer to a new access path over authoritative data is an INDEX,
-- which Postgres maintains on write, transactionally, always correct. It is not a copy of
-- the data that a human has to remember to regenerate. That distinction is the whole reason
-- this is being deleted rather than repaired: derived-and-auto-maintained is a read; derived
-- and-hand-maintained is a cache, and a cache over the substrate's own content is a bandaid
-- over the absence of the right index.
--
-- Order matters: the closure function references the table, so it goes first. The table was
-- registered with pg_extension_config_dump, so dropping it also removes it from the
-- extension's dump set -- intended, since there is nothing left to restore.
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS laplace.constituents_closure_edges(bytea[], integer);
DROP PROCEDURE IF EXISTS laplace.constituent_edges_rebuild(integer);
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS laplace.constituent_edges;
